silent movie references political satire  conveniently timed lightning strikes  here are a few things about the 1985  sci-fi classic back to the future that  you'll only notice after watching it  multiple times  a lot of the foreshadowing in back to  the future isn't exactly subtle but some  bits are easier to miss than others for  example when marty mcfly worries about  sending out his audition tape in the  film's opening moments he speculates  about the record company telling him he  has no future sure enough he almost  doesn't if you look at some of these  early moments long enough you find out  how layered they really are especially  once you know the course that the movie  is going to take in the scene where  principal strickland scolds marty for  being a slacker it's easy to notice that  he says that history's gonna change but  he also identifies george mcfly's lack  of drive or lack of backbone really as  the reason marty's father never amounted  to anything sure enough that turns out  to be the exact part of history that  changes  it's always been obvious that the last  few minutes of back to the future serve  to set up the plot of part two but  there's one bit of sequel bait that only  gets a double take once you already know  what'll happen when marty gets to 2015.  right before the libyan nationalists  crash the party doc brown mentions his  high-minded time travel goals i've  always dreamed of seeing the future  looking beyond my ears  seeing the progress of mankind  very noble and inspiring indeed but then  he adds something that most of us would  probably find even more inspiring i'll  also be able to see who wins the next 25  world series  it's a fine joke but it's a lot more  foreboding in the context of biff's use  of the sports almanac of film later plus  it makes doc's outrage about marty's  identical idea for a money-making scheme  in part two kind of hypocritical then  again the doku gleefully hoped to learn  the world series winners isn't really  the same doc who scolded marty for  besmirching the nobility of time travel  maybe the extra 30 years he had to  speculate on probable consequences or  able to change his mind if you're a fan  of silent comedies few images are as  iconic as poor harold lloyd dangling  from the hand of an enormous clock in  1923's safety last if you know the film  or even just the shot it's impossible  not to see the resemblance when doc  brown finds himself hanging for dear  life from the hour hand it's a great  homage especially when you remember that  doc is played by yet another albeit  unrelated lloyd christopher this time  lloyds of the world should probably all  stay away from clock towers just in case  back to the future isn't content with  just one reference to safety last though  it sneaks in a second one that doubles  as foreshadowing for doc's later bit of  aerial comedy in the film's opening as  the camera pans over doc's clock  collection we see that one of the clocks  has a tiny figurine of harold lloyd  attached to one of the hands we take it  to doc brown as a fan of safety last  himself  no one turns on a classic time travel  caper to see a small town mayoral race  but you still shouldn't miss this  cynical joke about the cyclical nature  of politics in marty's original 1985  timeline mayor goldy wilson is running  for re-election and the megaphones  installed on his campaign van make it  clear that progress is his middle name  political and civic progress that is  because the slogan certainly isn't  referring to creative progress back in  1955 mayor red thomas has his campaign  car blare the exact same slogan his  predecessor almost certainly followed  exactly the same script if you're paying  enough attention you might notice that  the two campaigns match each other word  for word but subsequent viewings give  you more time to appreciate the layered  ironies at play another favorite both  mayoral candidates are incumbents their  campaigns are promoting change as the  reason to keep on re-electing the same  guy  there's been plenty of nostalgia for the  80s over the past decade just as the 70s  and 80s had plenty of similar nostalgia  for the 50s back to the future was part  of that wistful cultural wave and the  set design proves it once upon a time  hill valley had more civic pride and  less vandalism the movie's view of this  nostalgia is nuanced however and it  doesn't hesitate to skewer exactly this  kind of longing for the past in back to  the future we see that the 50s were far  from innocent after all both goldie  wilson and the band playing the school  dance get racist slurs thrown at them  just for existing the hill valley of  1955 may not have had an adult movie  theater in the town square but it still  had rapists and peeping toms compared  with all that marty's fantasy of a night  away at the lake with the steady  girlfriend seems positively wholesome  even the most basic bits of nostalgia  get subtly taken to task here idealized  family dinners as soon as they get the  chance lorraine's family is thrilled to  watch tv while they're eating just like  the mcflies in the 80s  life imitates art so when an  unrecognizable vessel crashes into your  barn and a mysterious figure in a  bizarre full bodysuit emerges you might  well think aliens are responsible  especially if it's 1955 and you're a kid  with a collection of comic books books  movies tv comics these things are just  part of how we relate to the world  around us back to the future knows this  and leans into it one of biff's pals has  the gimmick of always wearing a pair of  red and blue 3d glasses around a nod to  the 50s craze for 3d which would  eventually return in marty's 80s and  again without glasses in the futuristic  2015 it's just one of several ways the  film shows that the more things change  the more they stay the same  however the best bit of meta-fictional  overlap in back to the future comes when  marty hooks up his camcorder to doc's  1955 tv to show him the footage taken  right before marty's desperate escape  through time  as marty and doc rewind and fast forward  to get to the parts they need in the  tape they're duplicating the time travel  process in their own way moving  backwards and forwards through marty's  future historical record  throughout back to the future marty  always seems to have the same problem  he's constantly late of course it  doesn't help that all the clocks and  doc's workshop are set to the wrong time  but those clocks have nothing to do with  him lingering at the dance when he knows  he has to make it to the clock tower on  time priorities marty we all like johnny  be good but you don't want to get stuck  in 1955  of course the ongoing detail of marty's  lateness gets a satisfying and briefly  bittersweet twist when marty finally  thinks to arrive somewhere early it's so  he can save doc's life marty giving  himself 10 additional minutes to get to  the mall parking lot is an excellent end  to his character arc in the first film  he may be late for everything else even  his own future but when his best  friend's life is at stake he's able to  step up and work past his most  unfortunate habit  when it comes to time nobody has any  business criticizing dr emmett brown he  invented the flux capacitor and  conquered the laws of physics while  everyone else is still frustrated about  daylight savings time however there's  one specific detail that it doesn't take  a doctorate to notice thanks to the save  the clock tower campaign marty knows the  exact time and date that the hill valley  landmark will be struck by lightning  sort of he knows the day hour and minute  but most lightning strikes are over the  same second they began marty has to hit  doc brown's wire at the exact right  moment and he has no idea which moment  that might be as doc pointed out earlier  there are a lot of fiddly little details  that have to be worked out exactly right  for marty to be able to make it back to  1985 but this one seems more impossible  than the rest it's a good thing that  once doc comes back from the far off  year of 2015 he's got a more reliable  trash based fuel source  at first glance marty mcfly and his dad  don't seem to have much in common after  encountering them both in 1955 even doc  brown can't quite believe that the two  are related  that's not true of course considering  that the whole plot of the film centers  around marty being george and lorraine's  biological son it's concrete proof of  the mcfly bloodline but it's not exactly  necessary against all odds the film  makes it clear that george and marty  actually have quite a lot in common  right down to their body language  even more meaningfully the two share  some identical anxieties about rejection  neither one of them can initially work  up the courage to submit their creative  work to a publisher or label that might  turn it down thankfully all the  father-son parallels get a heartwarming  payoff in the film's final act marty  helps george find his courage and win  over lorraine and seeing his father  stand up for himself gives him the  motivation he needs to take over for  marv and barry and lose himself in the  music and when marty gets gawked at by a  bunch of 50s teens and teachers who  can't believe what they're hearing it  turns out that he can take rejection  pretty gracefully after all  kids and their parents have always  clashed over music the art form might  change over time but once our tastes are  fixed they often stay the same sooner or  later we're all bound to get confused by  the latest hits needless to say marty  experiences this phenomenon firsthand  when he plays an 80s style guitar solo  for a baffled 50's crowd i guess you  guys aren't ready for that yet  but your kids are gonna love it  that's not the only time back to the  future shows older generations suffering  when their kids crank up the volume on  the newest hits when marty dons his  hazmat suit to scare the 1955 version of  his dad into asking out lorraine he amps  up the weirdness factor by blasting  george with a few seconds of eddie van  halen cranked up to 10.  george writhes in agony as he's  literally tortured by marty's taste in  music he doesn't get it but his son sure  does  george mcfly's burgeoning career as a  novelist is the perfect cherry on top of  his happier better adjusted life in the  altered 1985. if you take a good look at  the cover of a match made in space you  might notice that it bears a resemblance  to those of real life science fiction  author robert a heimlich connections  between george and heinlein are likely  anyway he's one of the authors george  would have absolutely read growing up  considering that the teenage george was  ready to skip the big dance so he could  watch science fiction theater although  george doesn't know it his own life  winds up being like something out of a  certain hindland story and not just  because he's living in a science fiction  movie himself heinlein's short story all  you zombies deals with how time travel  paradoxes could lead to a person  becoming several of their own ancestors  for example in a decidedly less  family-friendly version of back to the  future we could see that happening to  marty too that's not the only hindland  story that echoes marty's journey though  heinlen's novel time enough for love  tackles multiple kinds of incest in a  science fiction setting including a  romance between a mother and her son  awkward doesn't even begin to cover it  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

